WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.100
The first film showed inherited beak differences spreading through unequal

2
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.340
reproductive success.

3
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.820
When does one changing population become two species?

4
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.140
Start with one mixed finch population.

5
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.920
Its members can meet, breed, and exchange gene versions across the island.

6
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:26.173
Biologists often use interbreeding as the test.

7
00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:32.253
A species includes populations whose members interbreed in nature and produce

8
00:00:32.253 --> 00:00:33.253
fertile offspring.

9
00:00:34.553 --> 00:00:36.273
They rarely breed with other groups.

10
00:00:37.513 --> 00:00:43.313
This biological species concept applies to sexual organisms, and its edges can be

11
00:00:43.313 --> 00:00:44.313
fuzzy.

12
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:46.893
Now a river divides the habitat.

13
00:00:48.553 --> 00:00:51.993
Birds on opposite sides meet less often, so gene flow falls.

14
00:00:53.413 --> 00:00:55.493
The river creates geographic isolation.

15
00:00:57.013 --> 00:01:01.473
Has the river already made two species, or has it only started a possible split?

16
00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:03.833
Make your prediction.

17
00:01:09.817 --> 00:01:12.297
Separation alone is not speciation.

18
00:01:13.677 --> 00:01:17.237
On the day the river appears, the birds may still breed if they meet.

19
00:01:18.997 --> 00:01:20.557
Distance describes where the groups are.

20
00:01:21.637 --> 00:01:26.157
The species test asks what happens when their members meet and try to reproduce.

21
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:31.940
Across many generations, different mutations may arise on each side.

22
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.440
Different conditions may favour different inherited traits.

23
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:39.440
Chance can shift small groups too.

24
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.460
The two colour distributions may move apart while reduced gene flow keeps

25
00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:46.940
differences from mixing freely.

26
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:53.700
Gene flow moves gene versions between populations when individuals, or sex cells

27
00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:56.780
such as pollen, arrive and add their genes.

28
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.300
Before the river, that mixing can keep the groups connected.

29
00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:06.560
After isolation, less mixing lets differences build more easily.

30
00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:13.480
Reduced gene flow allows divergence; it does not prove that divergence or speciation

31
00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:13.920
occurred. or sex cells, can keep the groups connected.

32
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:14.820
It does not prove that divergence or speciation occurred.

33
00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:18.253
Within each group, the same selection rule still works.

34
00:02:19.793 --> 00:02:23.713
Inherited traits spread when their carriers leave more reproducing offspring.

35
00:02:25.013 --> 00:02:28.433
The environment can make different traits helpful on each side.

36
00:02:29.673 --> 00:02:34.153
This can support divergence, but selection does not define the species boundary.

37
00:02:35.317 --> 00:02:37.237
Imagine the barrier disappears.

38
00:02:38.597 --> 00:02:43.717
If the groups interbreed regularly and exchange genes, the split is incomplete under

39
00:02:43.717 --> 00:02:50.097
this concept. If barriers prevent interbreeding, or allow only rare hybrids, the

40
00:02:50.097 --> 00:02:51.857
groups count as separate species.

41
00:02:53.077 --> 00:02:54.897
That is reproductive isolation.

42
00:02:56.067 --> 00:02:58.187
Real boundaries can remain fuzzy.

43
00:02:59.527 --> 00:03:04.827
Some named species hybridise occasionally, and the concept does not fit organisms

44
00:03:04.827 --> 00:03:06.387
reproducing without sex.

45
00:03:07.927 --> 00:03:10.367
Speciation can also begin without a barrier.

46
00:03:11.687 --> 00:03:15.847
The river is one useful model, not a rule that every lineage follows.

47
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.340
So a barrier can start a split by reducing gene flow.

48
00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:26.020
Mutation, selection, and chance may then make populations diverge.

49
00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.520
Interbreeding tests whether the split is complete.

50
00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.340
The same mechanism that changes populations can help separate them across

51
00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:35.980
generations.
